A||N||4||Pickett||Questions on Fieros||||5||
Z||000000||Pickett||04-30-2000||10:16 PM||||A little help please.  I know nothing about Fieros.  My cousin told me to consider them, that they are good cars.  I can't find any research about them from the usual car mags so what can you tell me about them?  Are they reliable?  Do they break down frequently?<P>I'm looking at two cars.  One is an '85 GT, auto with 67k for $2950; the other is an '86, 4 spd, with 81k for $3200.  Are these good prices?  which is the better deal?  What should I be looking for?  Will I regret getting one of these cars?<P>thanks for your help!!<P>Bob (from Wisconsin)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||04-30-2000||10:32 PM||||First of all, you have come to the right place!<BR>Consider that the newest Fiero is at least 12 years old. Any car that old is going to need some TLC. If you are not mechanically inclined, or if you have reservations about working on your own car you might want to pass on the Fiero. (flame proof suit going on) Fieros are "quirky" little cars and you pretty much have to have one to appreciate it. If you are sure that you want one, but not sure about working on it, get the one with the automatic. The manual tranny's clutch has been known to give hydraulic problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>Keep us posted on what you decide.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-30-2000||10:57 PM||||Get both cars VIN and go get a Carfax report. <A HREF="http://www.carfax.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carfax.com</A>  I think<P>Also run the VIN by 1800PMCARES or a dealer and see if the recall work was done.<P>do this before buying any used car. not just a fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-30-2000||11:00 PM||||As with all cars, the Fiero has it's good points and bad points. The better the maintenance has been during the lifetime of the car, the better the car (usually). We love our Fieros, they are a very unique little sports car and a pleasure to drive. Not to mention they look great. My advice is to shop around, take some test drives. As for holding their value and desirability, generally the 88 models are more sought after and the 84 are down on the bottom of the heap. The fastback GT's (86-88) are also considered by many to be "the right" Fiero, although I actually prefer the notchback style (which are much cheaper).<P>Lots of us on the Forum do our own work for several reasons: 1) We are, for the most part, car enthusiasts...Fieros are sort of a hobby for some. 2) Many "regular" mechanics don't know much about them and many won't work on them 'cause "they're different". 3) We hate spending money on labor, it takes cash away from our Fiero modification program. 4) We hate letting anyone else touch our baby for fear they'll screw it up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||04-30-2000||11:49 PM||||Whut Batboy said!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  Cooter...Such blasphemy!! (You were obviously asking fer it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>and ogre...er...(Ozzy too scared to comment for fear of ogre rolling a "Giant rock" on top of him <I>again</I> !) <P>My Fiero's are lots of work but when they run good...there is nuthin' else quite like it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> These folks here are an incredible information resource and can help you thru anything. I would hazard a guess to say that there is NOTHING that has been done to a Fiero that someone here has not done.<P>Not to mention the incredible humorous contributions on this forum,such as mine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||1
